The Prague Stock Exchange fell into a minus at the end of trading
After the morning slump, the Prague Stock Exchange corrected the losses.
Transactions with CEZ shares amounted to almost half of a regular daily trade.
Right from the beginning of Tuesday's trading, the Prague Stock Exchange continued its slump, falling by almost six per cent.
This time there is a slump in shares on Wall Street.
In our country, too, there is a reaction of the market to the outcome of the vote of the U.S. Chamber of Deputies, which refused to support the stabilization of the local financial sector.
Equities fall in Asia
Stock exchanges experienced a dramatic downfall on Tuesday, although the indices eventually erased some of the losses over the course of the day.
Hong Kong's Hang Seng index wrote off nearly four per cent during trading, but later erased some of the losses and reduced the decline to about 2.5 per cent.
The Hang Seng China Enterprises index, which tracks the movement of Chinese stocks on the Hong Kong stock exchange, fell 3.8 per cent, markets closed in Shanghai.
Stock market shares lost more than five per cent, but ultimately cut losses to 4.3 per cent.
According to the index, the stock exchange fell 3.6 per cent.
"The timing of a bailout in the U.S. is inseparable and it will affect the financial markets around the world," said Joseph Yam, head of Hong Kong's monetary policy.
Hong Kong, although it is part of China, determines its monetary policy on its own, undependiminator on China's central bank.
Hong Kong has interest rates at the same level as the U.S.
U.S. legislocators, according to Australian Prime Minister Kevin Rudd, should urgently return to negotiations and approve the proposal to support the financial system.
Otherwise, there is a risk that other countries will also feel the effects.
The Bloodbath of U.S. Stocks
The U.S. Chamber of Deputies on Monday declined a plan to support the financial system for which up to $700 billion (nearly CZK 12 trillion) should have been spent.
The legislators ignoring President George W. Bush's call to support the plan.
According to Bush, the plan would address the underlying causes of the financial crisis and help stabilise the entire economy.
U.S. equities experienced a bloodbath on Monday and major stock indices recorded the biggest fall in more than 20 years.
The Dow Jones index fell by nearly seven per cent and the slump in a similar slumstill last recorded in 1987.
The index was already deccumerated before the poll, but as soon as it turned out that the bill did not pass, the index found itself in a vulnerability.
U.S. U.S. government can pump $700 billion into banks
U.S. Congress' top officials and the administration of President George W. W. Bush have agreed on a broader form of an agreement on financial assistance for the U.S. financial system.
Voting will take place early next week.
U.S. legislators made a breakthrough in talks to approving a bailout plan in the form of $700 billion in financial assistance for the U.S. financial system (approximately $12 trillion).
However, it has not yet been won.
Congressmen must complete some of the details of an agreement before they are able to publish the final form of the law and vote on it.
The plan of support of the financial system will be debated in the Chamber of Deputies on Monday.
This was announced to Reuters on Sunday by Barney, the chairman of the Banking and Financial Services Committee of the Chamber of Deputies, Barney Frank.
According to the sources, there could probably be a vote on a plan in the senate at the earliest on Wednesday at the earliest.
According to economists, there should be announcing that the rescue plan will be the first significant psychological factor for the recovery of financial markets.
After that, however, there will be "sobrimming down" due to the complexity of the mechanisms by which to help the markets to achieve even in practice.
Paulson: The plan must be effective
“We have made a lot of progress.
“We’ve dealt out our differing views on what the shape of a market stabilisation package should look like,” Democrat Nancy Pelosi told Bloomberg.
According to her, the final vote could still take place on Sunday.
Legislators met with U.S. Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson last night to give a definite form to a government fund.
It should repurch repurchase unresupervisable mortgage assets from financial companies that are withdrawing financial firms into heavy losses and threaten the stability of the entire system.
"We are on the verge of a definitive agreement of a plan that will work and must also be effective on the market.
We still have to continue the recovery plan, but I don't think we're there," Paulson said.
Signal for asian trading
The global financial crisis is significantly impacting stock markets that are declining sharply.
According to non-Davwegian Democratic senator Harry Reid, legisors are thus trying to reach a final agreement in Congress on Sunday.
They want reassuencie investors ahead of trading in Asian financial markets, which are in the time lag first, where a congressional's decision could affect Monday's trading.
However, it is still uncleatakably clear when the two U.S. congressional chambers will vote on the law, or whether they couldn’t tick off a last-shift deal.
The legislators are hoping it will be approved over the next few days.
However, the law still undergoes a number of changes.
The total amount intended to help the system should be divided into two parts.
An initial $350 billion will be available as soon as possible, as requested by President George W. Bush.
However, Congress can block the release of the remaining amount if it gives the impression that the program does not fulfil its function, in a succession of an additional $ 100 billion and later a final $350 billion.
Bush praised the progress in the talks
The president can veto this decision, but Congress can override his veto.
In spite of these changes, however, the basic idea of the program, which was to raise funds to repurchase poor mortgage securities whose value has plummeted, because hundreds of thousands of Americans are unable to repay their Mortgage are retained.
"We've worked everything out.
The Chamber of Deputies should be able to vote on the law on Sunday and the Senate on Monday," Republican senator Judd Gregg said in a statement.
U.S. President-Chief President Bush is also satisfied with the negotiations.
Its spokeswoman, Tony Fratto, has stated that Bush is satisfied with Saturday's progress and appreciates "bo-partisan efforts to stabilise our financial markets and protect our economies."
Reducing golden parachutes
President of the Senate Banking Committee Christopher Dodd, the head negotiator, said the legislators had agreed to be accounted for the monetary program, the protection of taxpayers from the losses, the closure of concessions, the timing of the financing of the plan.
The new law is also intended to curb the so-called golden parachutes for directors participating in the program and setting up a committee to oversulerate the fund.
It should be managed by the Treasury Department.
"We've worked very hard on this and we've made a lot of progress towards an agreement that's going to work and it's going to be useful to the market as well for all Americans," Paulson said.
The plan also envisages help for homeowners who have problems with repayments.
The government should nenegotiate new repayments for the mortgages it will buy in order to reduce the monthly payment costs for borrowers and enable them to keep their houses.
Marek: Psychological help
The proposed plan, according to Patrio Finance Chief Economist David Marek, is a good idea, but its implementation will be very difficult.
"And that's because we don't know what mechanism and at what price troubled assets will be redeemed.
This will be decisive for how the financial sector and the government will share the losses among themselves," Marek told news server iHNed.cz this week.
Whether the plan will be approved now, or at the beginning of next week, it is not so important, according to Marek.
"Much it's much more important for the Congregators this week to agree that they're supporting this plan and that they're going to sanctify it as soon as possible.
The psychological importance of an agreement to be approved is the most important at the moment," Marek stressed.
Fight on the knife edge: MP3 anti-mobile players.
Advising on how to choose
While almost every mobile phone can play MP3 files, no MP3 player is able to make a phone call.
From this it seems that it is better to just buy a phone.
Nevertheless, there are enough reasons for a person to buy a separate player.
Choosing completely depends on the method of use and the demands of the future owner.
It's probably hard to get a top-of-the-range gramophone at home to listen to the latest album Maxim Turbulence.
On the one hand, such music products do not come out on LP boards and from a qualitative point of view there is also no objective reason.
However, if vinyls with art rock, jazz or blues crunch on your shelves, you are probably at least dreaming of such a gramophone.
It's the same with on-the-flight music, i.e. music compressed, in a sisshow - music in MP3.
Here, too, there are cost-of-quantuminary categories.
For some, it may be unacceptable to spend eight thousand crowns on the player, just like a Pink Floyd fan, it's probably hard to bite headphones for twenty crowns from the market.
Two extremes, among which there is enough space for everyone.
Choosing is wide.
Decides even battery life
Some high-end-of-the-art music players can do a lot more than just playing music, but what most cellphones don’t happen.
No simultaneous device is as versatile as a phone.
In Addition to primary functions, telephony and e-scenes, it can play music and video, record both, has a better, worse camera and maybe even navigating.
And when we consider smartphones and communicators, you will also have a small computer in your pocket.
It's a small miracle, because no refrigerator can't be grilled, and there's not a TV photographing anything.
Versatality of the mobile also brings disadvantages.
The more functions you use on your mobile, the sooner the indicator on the display will send you to the charger.
And music playback is one of the biggest grices of energy.
Few mobile phones can replay music for more than ten-twelve hours, mostly it's much less.
In this respect, the music players (especially the simplest ones) are significantly more powerful.
For inexpensive mobile phones, therefore, be aware that they won’t play for you for more than a few hours.
If you still make a call here and there, get one more charger to work - this also applies to more expensive devices.
You won’t have this problem with MP3 players, even the worse ones will last at least 9 hours of continuous operation on a single charge.
Another problem arises when uploading the music to your mobile phone.
Unfortunately, only a limited part of the devices has a universal USB connector, and moreover, not each of them can be directly connected to a computer without special software.
Of course, it is possible to record music directly on a memory card, but only a few card readers can co-release the miniature memory cards that are used in mobile phones without reduction.
You don't need any special software for players with exceptions (iPod).
It is enough to fill the device with music via USB from the computer.
Most mobile phones and players already create a music library sorted according to the name of the performer, the name of the album, the genre or, for example, the evaluation of the user.
Some devices only show the tree structure of directories similarly to Windows Explorer, and some can do both (iriver, Samsung, Sony, Cowon, smart phones with an operating system).
It's easier to operate, cellphones can do more
When selecting a specific device, be especially interested in battery life, memory capacity, quality and type of audio output and, of course, the comfort of control.
If the music is above all a beanie and if you listen to it every day only briefly on the tram, you just need any mobile phone that will reshuffle the MP3.
Almost any cell phone can do that.
You don’t even have to keep an eye on what music formats a mobile phone or player.
MP3 can handle everyone, and the vast majority of the music that gets to you will be in this format.
It is one of the most widespread and, in addition, the vast majority of devices can handle WMA music.
Fewer occurs AAC (format used by Apple and its iPods).
Better mobile phones also allow, for example, the purchase of on-the-line music.
You just need to visit the relevant pages of the operator (or other provider) over the mobile internet and buy a song or album.
This service in itself could already be a strong argument for the acquisition of a musical mobile phone.
Unfortunately, the offer of operators is miserable and it is mostly limited only to fast-leaned radio hits.
Primary is also not the lowest.
However, it is generally assumed that this way of selling music (whther via GSM, the Internet or wireless internet) is dominated by the music industry in the future.
Mobility most people always carry with each other.
Every time you want to listen to something, you can feel your phone in your pocket, while an MP3 player lies forgotten at home.
However, it also depends on whether you also always carry headphones or at least wired hands-free.
If you don't, you can take advantage of an integrated loudspeaker, which has almost every mobile phone, but only a minimum of MP3 players.
Probably the biggest advantage of musical mobile phones will be the price for many.
The cheapest ones usually cost under three thousand.
For three and four thousand kroner you can already get a very decent walkman.
However, you will usually be constrained by the absence of an ascent into regular headphones.
The finest and technically most well-equivoted phones even with a 3.5 mm casing for ordinary headphones costs up to fifteen thousand.
The advantage of the players is, among other things, easy operation.
The simplest - not necessarily the inexpensior (see our choice) - players can be handled really by anyone.
The minimum functions, the similarity of the control with a previously common cassette or a gramophone is obvious.
There is no risk of a collision in the form of accidentally switched-on mobile internet and subsequently a giant account for data transmission.
what device to reach for
Even the cheapest mobile phones (e.g. Vodafone 527) usually have an inter-restorable memory card slot (M2, microSD type, etc.).
Even with the purchase of the appropriate card you can fit into two thousand crowns.
However, be careful.
For the basic models, only cards with a maximum capacity of 2 GB are supported.
Only the most expensive ones can cope with 8 GB of memory.
If you already have a mobile phone and want to buy a cheap MP3 player, reach for Cowon, Creativ, Sony, Samsung, Emgeton or iriver.
Depending on the capacity (max. 2 GB), you do not have to spend more than 1 100 kroner.
If you are one of the more demanding listeners, rather reach for specialized mobiles - walkmanes (Sony Ericsson, some Nokia and Samsung).
These appliances have separate control buttons for MP3 and the comfort of operation is thus approaching a standalone MP3 player.
The furthliest is Sony Ericsson, whose walkman-seater mobile phones have an identical user interface as self-soluble MP3 players.
Efficient service and in the same price category up to four thousand crowns, but with a larger memory capacity (up to 16 GB) and battery life (usually 15 hours, up to a maximum of 35 hours), they will divert MP3 players from the above-mentioned brands.
The first virtual operator will arrive in the Czech Republic already this year.
Possibly
The mobile market in the Czech Republic is quite advanced and our operators offer practically all services as their partners in Western Europe.
Nevertheless, there is something missing here - a virtual operator that would force the classic ones to be cheaper.
He might not be missing for a long time.
We’ve written many times on Mobil.cz that Czech operators is one of the most expensive in Europe.
After all, ČTÚ also confirmed it, which pointed out that the price of SMS in our country is higher than in the rest of the Union.
After the European Commission's last-developed actions in the field of regulating cruising fees, there may be a paradoxical situation - for some customers of Czech mobile operators, SMS from abroad will be cheaper than at home.
How is it possible that prices in the Czech Republic stay so high compared to Europe?
The answer is simple, there is a lack of competition, which would really like to start a fight.
It may sound paradoxically, after all, we have three mobile networks and a penetrating high of over 100 per cent.
On the contrary, mobile operators like to pretend that the Czech market is extremely competitive.
Pavel Dvořák, Chairman of the Council of the Czech Telecommunication Office (ČTÚ), who in the past has repeatedly stated that there is no place for another operator in the Czech Republic.
I guess it's probably true that a classic operator with its own network doesn't seem to have too many chances anymore (after all, it would probably be difficult to look for frequencies anymore).
However, there is one more option - a virtual operator.
The entry of virtual operators into the mobile market brought a significant decrease in prices, to which classical operators also had to respond.
At the same time, prior to the entry of pikes such as Tesco Mobile and Virgin Mobile, there were similar votes in the United Kingdom in the United States.
But it turned out that the place for such operators is and can be really successful.
After all, we don’t have to go too far for examples.
In February of this year we informed you about the start of virtual operator Mobiking in Poland.
In just half a year, this operator managed to acquire more than 100 thousand customers and become the fifth largest operator.
Behind Mobilking stands the financial group Penta, which in the Czech Republic owns U:fona, among other things.
The advantage of virtual operators is the opportunity to focus on a much more specific target group.
In order to be able to function effectively, they only need far fewer customers than a classic operator.
They do not entail the relatively high costs for the construction, operation and maintenance of the mobile network.
In the world, virtual operants are usually profiled as low-duty - they offer inexpensive calls and SMS, but it is often balanced by a narrower offer of services.
For example, Tesco Mobile in the U.S. UK offers SMS at least half as cheap as its competitors.
However, as Mobilking in Poland shows the example of Mobilking in Poland, it can be quite a good move even a bet on a fresh target group.
This operator focuses specifically on men.
"Administrable guys are choosing Mobilking - an operator they like and trusted," says David Seibertz, CEO of the Mobile Entertainment Company, operating this virtual operator.
It's hard to say whether a similar concept as in Poland could be caught in our country.
Miroslav Čepický, spokeswoman for Vodafon, said: "The Czech market is relatively small for a segmented approach on which the majority of virtual operators are founded.
In addition, it is relatively saturated in terms of the supply of services."
In the Czech Republic without a chance, mobile operators say
However, it seems almost certain that if there will be a virtual mobile operator in the Czech Republic, Mobilking won’t.
"We talk about the expansion of this successful project, but in the Czech Republic the negotiations do not look very hopeful.
Rather, Mobilking will be triggered in other countries of our region," Jana Studničková, spokeswoman of the Penta financial group, said in a telephone interview for Mobil.cz.
Inadvertently confirmed that the current mobile operators in the Czech Republic are definitely not inclined to this idea.
On the other hand, they are trying to do everything so that no potential competitor arises.
For a long time, GTS Novera does not hide its interest in launching a virtual mobile operator, which would like to take the next step towards becoming a real competitor of Telefónika O2 in the field of corporate customers.
"We have a great interest in being able to offer our customers not only fixed but also mobile telecommunications services.
And the sooner the better.
We have repeatedly negotiated this possibility with all Czech mobile operators.
We have already managed to move forward with some of them in the negotiations,” says Pavel Kaidl, spokesperson for the group.
Maybe it's flashing for better times.
From unofficial information, it seems that the first virtual operator should be expected perhaps already by the end of the year.
For the time being, however, its origin is accompanied by an impenetrable wall of silence.
"At the moment, we do not believe that there is room for the creation of a full-fledged virtual operator on the Czech market.
The Czech market is very specific compared to other markets.
So far we have not been approached by any entity with a comprehensive and serious project in this area," Vlastimil Sršeň of the Telefónika O2 press department told Mobil.cz.
The specificity of the Czech market is also invoked by other operators.
T-Mobile press spokeswoman Martina Kemrová adds in a scarce agreement with O2: "In the meantime, we have not noticed a viable and mutually advantageous business project on which it would be possible to rebuild."
He adds: "It's going to be hard for a virtual operator to appear in the T-Mobil network by the end of the year.
At this point, we would already have to have a specific agreement on the table in order to be able to contemplate in this short term."
Vodafone pays its attention rather to the fulfillment of the requirements of its own 3G license.
Miroslav Čepický says: "By negotiating with potential virtual operators, we are open, especially in the area of cost-sharing and infrastructure.
This is what we consider to be the most tributable in terms of our customers.
Vodafone's main strategy in this area is to find a partner to build a shared network for a fast mobile internet."
Responsible authorities - with operators one hand
The procedure of mobile operators can be easily understood, the entry of another operator would mean an increase in competition, it would lead to a decrease in prices and thus a decrease in their gains.
So why make it easier for such a competitor?
The fact that when the operators care about something, they are able to pull together, is also evidenced by tumbling around the emergence of U:fon.
Probably the greatest pressure towards ČTÚ went at the time of U:fon from T-Mobile.
This is evidenced by publicly accessible information from CTU's website.
But it's U:fon that's going to come as an argument why you don'n't acquaint a virtual operator.
Martina Kemrová commented: "The competitive environment of the mobile market was recently further enhanced by the entry of a fourth mobile operator on the market.
With regard to the nature of the mobile market in the Czech Republic, it is clear that the entry of another player would put high demands on him and, above all, for his services."
Due to this approach of mobile operators, however, it is directly striking the complete lack of interest of Czech regulators.
It is not just the position of CTU, which unfortunately in the Czech Republic - unlike the United Kingdom and other developed countries - does not have the power to interfere in competition.
They belong exclusively to the Office for the Protection of Competition (ÚOHS).
However, he took his typical stance on the whole issue.
"A virtual operator's access to the mobile market would be possible after a contract was concluding between the mobile operator and the virtual operator for renting the necessary infrastructure.
No such treaty has been concluded so far, because the offer of potential bidders probably was not sufficiently interesting for classical operators," Kristián Chalupa from the Department of External Responsibilities of the ÚOHS said in response to our question.
It probably does not come as arguing from the representatives of the operators thus formulated.
From the office, which is supposed to represent the interests of consumers and, among other things, to make sure that no one takes advantage of their position on the market, it is more than considered.
Especially when Kristián Chalupa adds: "At abroad, where virtual operators normally operate, we can observe that their entry into the market has contributed to a reduction in prices for mobile telephonic services."
But apparently the pressure for the correct functioning of the market environment and a fall in prices do not belong to the interests of the Office.
Quite shocking on the whole case is the touching conceiness of three mobile operators, resulting from their reactions.
The question of whether it is really just a coincidence is offered on its own.
As well as exactly the same gasoline price of gasoline at gas stations, it is certainly only the work of concluding and a fierce competitive struggle.
"On this case, the Office did not find signs of anti-competition and therefore has no reason to intervene in any other way.
None of the operators has a dominal position, so they do not have an adequate increased responsibility for their actions on the market.
Negotiable tools for the opening of the mobile market for virtual operators are probably lacking to the telecommunications regulator," Chalupa said in a statement.
Interpretation of the dominant position on the market is one of the many contentious points.
Of course, there is an analysis of the relevant markets drawn up by CTU, according to which telecommunications from the point of point of view of competition is the only market.
On such a market, of course, none of the Czech operators have a dominant position.
However, some legal experts offer a different point of view - it is always necessary the consent of the network operator to create a virtual operator.
And the mobile operator - quite logically has a monopoly on its network.
Therefore, in a certain segment it necessarily has to be dominant.
However, even if we did not concede this argument, it should be at least a warning to the Office that the three mobile operators act conspicuously similarly.
At the same time, complaints from a number of interested parties (including GTS Novera) show that when dealing with all operators, they encounter very similar obstacles.
Complimination of responsibilities on ČTÚ is a relatively common trick in telecommunications, through which ÚOHS puts his hands away from the whole matter.
We have already encountered him in several similar cases.
Unfortunately, until the recently adopted amendment to the Telecommunications Act, the extension of CTU’s competences in telecommunications could not be enforced, even though many experts hoped for an improvement in the situation.
This must be known more than well, but apparently their interests are elsewhere.
It's good when no one's looking
At the same time unnoticed remains the functioning of pseudo-virtual operators, of whom there are several in Czech mobile networks.
They are companies issuing their own price lists of mobile telephone services, which are only partially public.
These are usually multi-level marketing companies.
Its Own mobile telephonic services is offered by Amway, Bohemia Telecom, Starlife and a number of others.
The price lists of these companies usually only go to the chosen ones.
The magic consists in the fact that the company acts against the operator as a classic corporate customer, who orders a large number of SIM cards.
As an important customer, he then receives a special price (and other) offers.
The problem is that the terms and conditions of all Czech mobile operators prohibit a similar resale.
"Should there be cases that you are talking about, it could be a violation of the contractual conditions that we would deal with and could result in the termination of cooperation with the company," Martina Kemrová said.
However, it is quite improbable that a similar activity will not be brought to the responsible people of mobile operators.
Especially in the case of the three mentioned companies it can be thousands of customers.
At the same time, none of the operators oppose such a procedure.
From the activities of these companies they have significant profits, and at the same time - unlike real virtual operators - they do not in any way jeopardize their own official marketing.
Miroslav Čepický draws attention to possible problems: "The law allows such speculation, but only a negligible percentage of customers use the services of these companies.
In addition, they have to accept that the SIM card in their phone is not in their name, so they can not fully dispose of it as regular customers of mobile operators.”
However, it should at least be odd CTU.
In addition, there is another question for the Office.
If mobile operators turn an eye on such a procedure, why do they block the formation of a virtual operator?
It should be added that the Office has relatively strong powers to detect such an activity, and if it does not want, it does not have to be satisfied with the explanation of the operators, that they have no idea about anything like this and their terms and conditions forbade it.
When won't we be the most expensive
As has already fallen several times, the entry of virtual operators into the mobile market brought a noticeable decrease in prices for services.
At the same time, in practically all the countries where virtual operators were created, the classical operators tried to hinder their emergence more or less.
The arguments themselves used were practically identical - too small or too saturated market, problems with the quality of the services, etc.
In the developed countries it was at these times that the regulatory authorities intervened, defending not the interests of mobile operators but their customers.
Whether by formal measures or informal pressure, they then achieved that the first virtual operator was created and others were loaded.
Unfortunately, the Czech regulatory authorities are tossing the whole case like a hot potato.
From our experience we can not get rid of the impression that the ambiguity in the competencies in these cases is more than useful.
But let's not throw flint into the rye.
If the behind-scene information is correct and the virtual operator will really appear in our country very soon, the Czech mobile market may be moving again.
If one of the operators admitted the emergence of a virtual mobile operator, it will hardly be able to remain indifferent to refutation (discriticizing) other interested parties.
And maybe we're going to stop shouting more expensive than in the rest of Europe.
The new Czech anthem has four new versions.
Choosing the best
Listen to four versions of the Czech national anthem.
Prime Minister Mirek Topolánek ceremonially presented on the occasion of the Day of Czech Magyarness on the occasion of the Day of the Czech State Worship, new recording of the national anthem Where My Home.
Hymne was newly recorded by the National Theatre orchestra under the leadership of Jiří Bělohlávka.
There are four versions - with masculine or feminine solo chants, in choral and instrumental versions.
Kateřina Kněínková and Adam Plachetka took over.
Otakar Jeremiáš and Jaroslav Krček are the authors of the anthem.
On iHNed.cz you can listen to all four versions.
Bělohlávek considers the Czech national song to be one of the most beautiful antheies.
Earlier, he has told Czech Radio that he deliberately selected singers with young representative voices, which are expressive and popular.
"From the very beginning I also tried to take care of the choir to have a clear dictation, so that the text should be well understood, to resonate," he said.
The Czech national anthem will commemorate the 175th anniversaries next year.
The song Where My Home was first sounded in 1834 on the boards of the Estates Theatre in the original Czech play Fidlovaček Josef Kayján Tyl complemented by the music of František Škroup.
It has gained popularity and people spontaneously took it as their own.
The first verse of the song then after the establishment of Czechoslovakia in 1918 became a natural part of the national anthem.
One of the seven state symbols remained even after the establishment of an independent Czech Republic.
As the "new" an anthem sounds
The Prague public transport will also ride a chip for the first time on Wednesday
On Wednesday, October 1, 2008, it will go down in the history of the Prague Public Transport Company as a date when the first passengers will enter the metro, trams and buses, who will have an electronic coupon in their pocket instead of a paper ticket.
At this point, about a hundred thousand people who own an Opencard chip card can be purchased.
Travellers can buy a 30-day and 90-day and annual coupon (which will be valid from January 1, 2009) and at the the same time have it loaded on their chip card in the information centers and workplaces of the Transport Company in the metro stations Muzeum, Můstek, Nádraží Holešovice, Anděl, Roztyly, Letňany, Kobylisy, Rajská zahrada, Kačerov and Luka.
Coupons can also be bought via the Internet from yesterday on the website of the Transport Company www.dpp.cz.
More than four hundred customers took advantage of the service in the after-afternoon.
Those who start using the online store can definitively forget dispensing windows in metro stations, where so far they had to regularly stand in long queues for paper tickets.
Coupon can be purchased over the Internet and it will be uploaded on the Opencard in one of the thirteen branches of the Municipal Library.
"At these places, there is a counter with trained service available to passengers, which will help those interested with an online purchase and will also upload the purchased coupon on their card," project spokesman Martin Opatrný said.
In addition to the libraries, it will be possible to upload electronic coupons on the Opencard also in self-service vending machines, which are mounted in several subway vestibules.
What do slot machines look like?
They are red, they are reminiscent of ticket markers and passengers may have already noticed them in Roztyly, Budějovická, I. P. Pavlova, Florenc and Vltavská metro stations.
"Now there are five of them in the city, but by the end of the year there should be eighty of them," Opatrný added.
Coupon will incite an automaton instead of the driver
Leaking slot machines that will start working tomorrow is simple.
It is enough to relocate an Opencard smart card and the previously paid coupon will be uploaded to it itself.
Electronic coupons from tomorrow apply not only in the capital, but also in its surroundings.
That is, on commemorative trains and bus routes with the number 300 to 499, through which the inhabitants of the Central Bohemian municipalities mainly go to the capital.
"On trains enrolled in Prague Integrated Transport, the conductors are equipped with card readers, through which they determine the time and zone validity of coupons," Filip Drápal from Ropid, which compiles the timetables for public transport in Prague.
Suburban buses have card readers built into the cash register at the driver's.
When boarding, it is therefore necessary not only to show the smart card to the driver, but it is also necessary to enjoin an Opencard to the designated place on the side of the cash register.
In this way, the driver will incite the validity of recorded coupons.
"If the passenger needs to verify the validity of recorded coupons, there is nothing easier than to ask the driver for this information," Drápal added.
