Sudetets ask U.S. for a referendum
Sudeten Germans in 1945 demanded the inclusion of Šumava towards the United States.
It follows from a letter that is deposited in the collections of the South Bohemian Research Library.
When we cannot be part of Germany or Austria, we want to live at least under the auspices of the United States of America.
With such a wish, some groups of Sudeten Germans allegedly turned to the President of the United States in 1945.
This is the result, among other things, from a letter that has been preserved in a private archive and is now deposited in the collections of the South Bohemian Scientific Library.
The Germans from Český Krumlov are requesting in a letter to the American president not to allow the "Šumava county" - occupied by Hitler on the basis of the Munich agreement - back to Czechoslovakia.
"We have correctly understood the situation and we really want to be sure that American troops are coming into German territory not as oppressors and enemies, but as liberators," reads the letter, signed with the words "The People Whose Desire the Descendance of Šumava."
Since annexation to Czechoslovakia supposedly gives the Sudeten Germans - knowledge of the Czech national nature - hope only for brutality, ruthlessness, misunderstanding, oppression and slavery, the authors of the American president's letter to take over Šumava to the Protectorate for the present and eternal times.
“We can’t believe that, regardless of the opinion of a quarter of a million inhabitants, this territory has admitted to a foreign state that oppresses and refutes minorities and does not contribute anything to an Allied victory.”
The reasons why the authors can not sign is allegedly the fear of vengeance of "Czech oppressors".
If the U.S. president allegedly doubts the mindset of Šumava Germans, it was enough to hold a referendum on whether its inhabitants want to become a part of the U.S. or live under the auspices of the United States in Šumava.
Although the text of the letter may sound a little naïve during today's knowledge of the post-war history, according to some witnesses, it appropriately illustrates the expectations of Sudeten Germans at that time.
Although the Czech Germans at least had to believe that it would be their expulsion from the republic, very few of them admitted such an option at all.
Some expected the U.S. and the United Kingdom to Negotiates a certain kind of autonomy for the Sudetenland.
Others believed that they would simply stay in Czechoslovakia.
This is also confirmed by the famous Austrian journalist Gustav Chalupa, born in České Budějovice.
During the war, he was a German-deployed to the navy at the Balt in Budweis.
At the end of the war he was captured by the British and placed him in a prison camp in Holštýn, dozens of other Czech Germans went to see him to teach them Czech.
"They believed that when they got home, they'd come in handy," Chalupa said.
According to him, none of the German captives, living before the war in Czechoslovakia, had been counting on being withdrawn out of the republic on the basis of Beneš's decrees.
