Coup dyes also have a feeling
When turkey or cockroach runs away from Danger, does this also affect fear?
Neurologist Joseph LeDoux is convinced that even many animals have feelings.
But how would it be possible to verify whether they felt the same when they behave similarly to us?
For several years, contributions from well-familiar scientists have been published in the "Edge online journal.
SPIEGEL ONLINE in its series presents selected answers to "What do you consider to be true without being able to prove it?"
I believe that animals have feelings and other states of consciousness, without me or anyone else still being able to prove when we cannot prove that other people have consciousness.
But in this case we can at least hope, because all human brains have the same basic configuration.
However, as soon as we begin to dedicate ourselves to a different kind and we open questions of perception or general consciousness, we are entering impervious terrain because the hardware doesn’t coincide.
When a turius goes into danger, it will do the same as many other animals: it will pretend that it is dead, it flees or attacks.
People behave similarly, from which some scientists conclude on comparable subjective experiences.
However, I don't believe that.
The two aspects of the brain's structure make it more difficult to transfer our subjective experience to the passing of animals.
On the one hand, circuits associated with the human consciousness almost always involve a side prefrontal cortex (depending on short-to-term memory and performance or control functions).
This large-scalculate area in humans is significantly more developed than in other primates, and it appears to be completely absent from other creatures.
At Least those aspects of our consciousness that depend on the pre-frontal cortex, among them the knowledge of who we are, and the ability to plan and make decisions, there is a reason to believe that a person differs even from other primates.
Another dramatic oddity could be hedded by the linguistic nature of a person: because such a large part of its perception is associated with the language, it is often said that consciousness depends on it.
In this case, we could explocate the animals.
But even if consciousness didn’t depend on the language, the language would certainly affect it, so the animal consciousness, no matter how it is engineered, probably differed from most of our consciousness states.
Therefore, it is difficult to find out what consciousness looks like in other animals.
When, as an intrinsic and subjective thing, it can not even be measured, for the sake of special hardware on the basis of our own experience, its exploration puts us faced with major problems.
Most of my outlets refer to the content of conscious experience, but in addition, there is another aspect of consciousness, which should be scientifically more accessible.
It would be possible to examine the processes in animal consciousness regardless of their content, and that is exactly what happens in short-term memorandum studies for primates, especially the example of visualization.
But this approach, to which Christof Koch and Francis Crick focused on neural collitus of consciousness rather than causal mechanisms: they can both be identical, but they may also not.
Interestingly, this approach also emphasizes the importance of a non-frontal cortex for visualization.
What does this imply because of feelings?
I deduce them from the fact that an emotional system, such as a system of fear, is activated in the brain-sustaining self-acquisition.
Thus, what we refer as "fear" is the mental state in which we find each other when the activity of the defense system localized in the brain (or its consequences, such as physical reactions) uses operating memory.
Seen from this angle, feelings are closely intertwined with those cortex areas characterised by primates, especially humans.
Interconnection with linguistic nature creates a subtle gradgenbilation of perception, because through words and grammar we can distinguish and categorize the states, in order to attribute them to others.
