Purely theoretically, I cannot contradict it, but I consider it unprovable. I consider it unprovable.
Pankseep argues argues that what appears to be a fear for rats and people is probably felt by both organisms as well: But how is it possible to verify, Pankseep further says whether rats and humans feel the same when they behave similarly?
Cockroach flounder from the danger - does he also feel the fear?
From my point of view, a mere remiiture of behavior cannot prove the congruence of experience.
It is true that neural comparison moves us further: rats and humans have similar cerebellous stems, on the other hand, cockroaches or brain.
But is the cerebral strain responsible for the feelings?
