Pride and Obstacles to the Life of Negroes in a Separate City
Regarded among the scattered amusement parks of the Greater Orland, a few miles from everything shiny, noisy and huge, lies a quiet city in which the pride and difficulty of Afro-American life comes to life.
Eatonville, the world’s first wholly-negroom city was incorporated into the state, where Zora Neale Hurston spent her childhood, is no longer as simple as described in 1935: “A city of five lakes, three croquet playgrounds, three hundred brown skins, three hundred good swimmers, plenty of kvayava, two schools and no imprisonment.”
Now it's a pilgrimage site.
Alice Walker, Toni Morrison and Ruby Dee have come to Zora's annual Acenville festival to pay tribute to Hurston, Harlem's most-familiar-maker's most-year-leading writer.
The city she described stays aside, even though she made it a stop for hikers to include him in a Florida tourist guide created by the Federal Writers project from the time of the economic crisis.
It is still as independent, dignified and bizarre as it was in the 1930s when Hurston wrote that country blacks in Florida often withstood and did not share their true feelings with a white man who "knows so little about us that they don't even know what he's missing."
Even today, in a year when the black presidential nominee, Senator Barack Obama is calling for an open debate about races, many residents here remain vigilant towards the views of strangers.
"We pay great attention to how our story is being told," says 59-year-old Hortense Jones, who has lived here since birth and is a member of the city's oldest parish.
"It has to be right."
Eatonville has long been characterized as a paradox of triumph and exertion.
It's both a historical model of a black-earth rise and a community of nearly 24,400 people where the poverty rate is twice that of the nationwide average.
It is a center of literature, but also an oak-shrined example of Southern rural black culture - sometimes despondactited, sometimes lauded - which arose from American slavery.
It is not very surprising that local residents are both proud and protective.
And worries about Eatonvilla really began with Zora, which is the only way Hurstonians are called here.
Introduced her hometown to the world through heartfelt books filled with dizziness such as "Mules and Men" (1935) and "Their Eyes Were Watching God."
Five paragraphs of the Florida Tourist Guide have turned the city a short distance into road 17, which leads through the often-dephemeral center of Florida, into a stage of black history and human drama.
With the vacancy of the big drum in her life and literature, Hurston introduced readers to the store of the first Acenville mayor, Joe Clark, and then turned to more personal places.
"Left next to the side of the road," she wrote, "is a white-decorated modern national school with well-maintained yards and playgrounds that Howard Miller always takes care of, even though he can't even write much."
She also mentioned Dash' Widow's new husband and wrote that Lee Glenn "sells drinks of all kinds and anything that fits an hour-to-hour hotel."
So in just a few Hundred Words, Hurston Eatonville has intertwined with self-government but also with illiteracy, reclosing of marriage and sex.
Clearly, it wasn’t a typical Fodor’s publishing house.
In fact, it wasn't a portrait everyone appreciated.
"Zera has said this as it was," says ninety-year-old Ella Dinkins, one of the choir of the Johnson Girls that Hurston has made immortal by quoting men sang rude songs about their beauties.
"Some people didn't like it," she added.
Hurston is still reminisced here as a pure ercentric who often came back after her family moved to Jacksonville, Florida.
Sixty-seven-year-old Augustus Franklin recalls that most of the time when she rushed into the city she arrived unannounced in huge Chevrolet, she smoked and wore pants in the city, which is still proud of dignified dressing.
Majority the population was fascinated, Franklin said, but many sneered each other.
"The people of Zor have always been happy to see," Franklin said.
But, he added, rocking on a chair on the back-deck overlooking Lake Sabelia, where Hurston was most likely baptized "neither she's ever stayed for too long."
When Hurston died in 1960, she was miserable and her books were removed from the press.
Eatonville, along with the rest of the world, seemed to have forgotten her.
Although she was once a literary star, a contemplation of Langston Hughes and the only black woman at Barnard College in the twenties of the 20's, she was buried in an unlabelled tomb in Florida's Fort Pierce where she had previously lived.
In Eatonvilla there were no major mourning ceremonies or large public readings.
"I don't think they didn't even understand its importance for the world or its legacy at all," Valerie Boyd, Hurston's biography author "Wrapped in the Rainbows" released in 2003.
The change came in the 80's of the 20th century.
Orange County officials wanted to stretch the city through a five-lane highway instead of Kennedy Boulevard, a two-lane two-lane main road.
Orland's sprawling was already pushing the inter-State Highway #4 through the western outskirts of the city.
The suggestion came when Eatonville kept coming from a difficult historical period.
Suspenished integration, along with other things, ended the relatively idyllic isolation of this community.
In the fifties of the 20th century, the struggle for mixing races brought hatred to the threshold of the community.
"At that time, a bunch of white boys, they came and threw oranges and things at people sitting on the side," Franklin said.
"Actually, we've had a lady who killed it.
Throw a memelon out of the car.'
In a 1955 letter to The Orlando Sentinel, Hurston questioned the Supreme Judicial Court’s request for compulsive integration, claiming that she was “offensible for my race rather than celebrating.”
Inhabitants now argue that the desegregation of the schools, although it was positive in some areas, reduced cohesion in Eatonville and underminudied the courage of the local youth.
"Charme children used to hugs - I remember - you hugged your teacher in the morning, you hugged your teacher in the evening," said N. Y. Nathiri, Ella Dinkins' daughter and executive headmi-treat from the non-in-non-non-non-non-profit Preserve the Eatonville Community (Save the Eatonville Community).
It lasted as long as the teachers and pudencies came from the same place, she added.
"You don't hug your white teacher because your white teacher - I want to say that there's a cultural barrier out there," Miss Nathiri said.
Civic rights, however, helped to create space for many other Zora Neale Hurston - black writers, actors and performers who, like her, uplifted the prejudices with reckless self-confidence and quotes such as "How can someone deny the pleasure of my presence!
I don’t understand.”
In 1975 Alice Walker traveled to Hurston's unlabelled grave and began to struggle to revive her reputation.
Five years later, a laudiated biography of Hurston by Robert E. Hemenway came into the shelves in bookstores, reinstating the American canon.
A highway project had just arrived when Eatonville's most-leainthest-year-old daughter found the ramp lights again.
Former Hurston neighbors have now regarded her as a savior.
In order to show what it could destroy with its highway, the community began planning the Hurston festival in 1988.
Thousands of fans arrived at an inaugural event two years later, and every January many come back to celebrate.
After a few years, the state withdrew from the highway proposal.
"Three streams of the highway resurrected, you'd say it blunted directly, real civic pride," Nathiri said.
Boyda said this more simply: "Zora saved Eatonville."
Winning the voyage project helped change the city's image.
Out-of-of-ambigures like Rachelle Munson, a lawyer who began attending a local church in 1993 began to appear in larger numbers, and residents began to re-evaluate the past.
Eatonville Joined the National Historical Registry in 1998.
A new single-story library (named after Hurston, of course), was opened in 2006 on a resphalted and beautified Kennedy Boulevard.
Eatonville remains a Florida an anomaly: just six miles from central Orlanda can sometimes act as a sunken alley in the summer rain, as small as when it was founded with just 27 black families in the 1980s in the 19th century.
(Today it's 90 per cent black.)
Strangers who come to look for the story and the importance of Eatonvill are still often treated prudencies.
Permit for most conversations is necessary to have in advance and certain things - as murmur paintings in the eldest Geonvill church, which have been painted by white people and depicting blacks in the fields - must not be photographed. They must not be photographed.
Many in Eatonville, like Jones, a courageous self-sufficient teacher who likes a deep red, are still worried that their isolated community won't be properly understood.
And even in the same way the Hurston festival shrugged, domestic pride has also heightened.
Particularly young people gravit urging Eatonville how Hurston has seen her entire race: beautiful, with problems and so on, but not better, not worse and equally proud, creative, diligence, foolish and diverse as other American races and ethnicities.
Franklin's nephew, eighteen-year-old Edwin Harvey, has an sincere love for the municipality when he plans to go back to Eatonville after college to work in a local government or the police she says she would take any help.
And even younger ones, such as eleven-year-old twins from Alondra and Alexio Kenon's Winter Park, seem to have learned to describe Eatonville correctly.
"Most people, if they just go through here, they'll think, 'Uh, this city is nothing compared to the others, 'Alondra Kenon said recently after the church.
"But if you really stop and look at history for a while, it's a very nice city."
Here in New York we are all fluctuating financially
Nothing has changed at first sight.
If tourists in Manhattan stayed away from newsstands and giant TV screens in Times Square, they wouldn't realize they were standing in the middle of a storm.
They would never have guessed that in the glazed bee hive of offices over their heads, bankers and investment fund managers monopolizing New York have the worst "wrong day in the office" since the 1929 crisis.
The thieves still rumble with metal carts on the pavements and carry the muffin bakers to breakfast meetings.
Hispanics are still shifting between the tower blocks and carrying, like human mules, the fifties of ironed shirts hanging from the honks on their back.
Office rats are still waiting queuing for their morning baguette at street dining stalls, and it's still not possible to get a yellow taxi between the fourth and seven o'clock in the afternoon.
It didn't look like a crisis at all, as long as I didn't decide to open a savings account on Thursday afternoon.
Even though I have timed this engineuent task to coincide with a late afternoon siesta at my local JP Morgan Chase branch, about twenty people were already in the queue.
It's not in front of the checkouts to pick up or deposit cash, but in front of the counter, where older clients are ordering chequebooks, hikers are asking for a Macy's convenience store, and new customers setting up bills.
Most of the people in front of me, as I learned from Starr, my "licensed personal banker", came for the same reason as me.
But compared to me, they have already withdrawn their savings from smaller banks where they came from, such as New Jersey, Pensylvania or Staten Island.
They have withdrawn money from so-called thrifts (a bit similar to building savings banks), cash associations and those types of savings banks whose branches only operate in a single state, claiming that they take care of their customers as a family.
In the past year, 15 such banks have gone bankrupt.
A few days ago Washington Mutual, $327bn asset bank, went into an audition, went bankrupt, and when the New Yorkers were coming home for the weekend Friday night, another bank, Wachovia, with $4.40 billion assets, tried to catch a bailout buyer.
The employees of the offices and shops who stood in front of me and were afraid of further collapsing shifted their cash to the bank, which is perceived as a safe harbor, which makes most of the bailout on Wall Street.
Ordinary New Yorkers have flourished financially fluctually over the past week.
A woman in my local Chinese supermarket was talking on the phone, expressing herself at Lehman Borthers, that bankrupt bank, and her emaciated chief executive Dick Fuld with the familiarity as if she was scratching a lazy comrade.
And Lyn, a uniformed roughly fifty-year-old caretaker of my tenement, changed the theme of our ordinary conversation - Brad, Angelina, or my food delivery.
After my visit to the bank, she told me: "I've just got my savings in money-to-market funds.
“I’ve moved it, I won’t take risks.”
