"To captive a true character, I photograph a master outside the scene." Günther Bauer
No heels, no ruffles or costumes.
Only the Essencies of the great flamenco masters detached away from concentrating on the interpretation - I just achieve the vacant expression of a man or a woman.
On the one hand an artist.
On the other - a man.
Inseparable, acknowledging privacy, in those moments far away from the audience - at that time you can see an intrinsic gesture, the depth of a glimpse view, or a passionate hand gesticulation which are carefree, because they are not going to ascend to the scene.
German Günther Bauer alternating in "Flamenco-people" theatricality with singers, dancers and guitarists with the lesser-known face of the masters.
With this inquisition, the photographer compiles a unique flamenco iconography from different environments.
The exhibition, which takes place as part of flamenco's program of the Femenco photography (Mes de la Fotografía Flamenca), "it reflects the ever-increasing international importance of flamenco", the author says, explaining that "Flamenco-people" "they can be any people, but when you look at the photographs, you will find that these are not typical images of artists on the stage.
These people portrayed "on the street" are artists capable of transferring passion, sadness, joy to the scene... and completely captivate spectators.
It does not have to be photographed on the stage to capturing the personality of these performers.
They carry their personality and character on the inside.
I was interested in the creation of a relaxed atmosphere, which will allow me to captivate the personality of flamenco greats.
Charmed by the "Cante de las Minas", Bauer began a long way to portray flamenco scoundrel singers, guitarists and dancers on a board camera.
These are portraits that, thanks to the choice of the camera and the material, reminiscent of classical photography.
Applying different stylistic means is inconsistent with the classical foundation of the image and gives it the contemporary spirit, an idea that, according to Bauer himself, reflects flamenco: "Music with very traditional roots, which does not cease to surprise and evolve."
What depresses spectators is not the action of the dance itself, singing or the game, but the personality and character of the artist.
The search for a moment when we encounter the most elapsed, the spirit that manifests itself in a way of perceiving, being, and experiencing, it was and is the constant of the work presented during these days in the El Fotómato gallery.
This selection of almost seventy photographed performers is formed by well-familiar flamenco characters such as Diego El Cigala, Hano Lobato, Cristina Hoyos, Enrique Morente, El Lebrijano, Antonio Canales and Vicente Amigo.
The project became an anthology of flamenco and will soon get a book edition, which will contain exhibited portraits.
Two years ago, the passion for portraiture led Bauer to the idea of repetition on "Portraits Sports Veterans" ("SportARTproject").
Although their "Flamenco-people" concept is different, the photographer admits that "to take pictures of people, to get to know them and have the opportunity for a moment, whether longer or just short, to come into their lives - this is my passion.
I don't think a portrait in photography is the most difficult genre.
A person meets or clashes with people, personalities they sometimes don't even even know, or only give him a few minutes to take a photo shoot.
Contrary to such circumstances, as a photographer, I always try to realize my ideas.”
