And so I think we'll just have to dream of a well-prepared Olympic city.
(Microsoft Is Looking for a Receptionist (virtual)
Speaking at the MIT-New MIT technology conference, Microsoft demonstrated further developments in a new image recognition system and human interaction on the way of artificial intelligence - not too intelligent, but able to perform basic tasks such as calling a taxi (or in the case of a Microsoft, a messenger who would move around the company's premises) and perceive what he hears in the conversation.
Thanks to the camera and a system that recognizes shapes and faces, this "receiver" can find out how many people there are in the room who they are - even to remember what they're wearing - and obeying and comprehensiving the keywords of their conversations.
It can also sort those present according to conversational groups.
In the demonstration video, the receptionist records a third person and understands that he does not belong to the group that talks to her, and that it is not an employee of the company.
He asks her if she's going to see someone, and she's telling her that he's going to take care of her in a moment.
All this without the blue screen of death.
Breathtaking.
Of course it is the music of the future.
Now the company has more important things to work, for example, a new version of the Windows operating system.
There's been a lot of talk this week about changing Microsoft's strategy.
Windows 7 will be lighter and they won't have as many associated programs.
Steve Ballmer found out that if the computers had only the most necessary, without a burden of commercial uselessness, they would be five times more powerful - man, Steve, after all, they would be cheaper.
Discover like thunder when we consider that Microsoft from version adds new features to Windows and another "extra".
A change of direction would be a meritorious act.
“Microsoft isn’t right, the operating system is slowly losing its meaning, and so is the software.
When Google Chrome appeared on the network, I became one of those leaning towards the theory that it was Google's first step towards a rival competition with Windows.
Many justifiably pointed out that the browser is not an operating system.
An operating system will always be necessary because it is a necessary stratum to comprehension between hardware and software.
It has the same function as your wife or your husband when you have to tell your mother-in-law.
That's the truth.
It will never go away, but it is necessary to think of the "death" of an operating system not as an absolute truth, but relative to the truth.
What Ballmer said this week confirms this idea.
People are paying less and less attention to the operating system and, conversely, the Internet browser.
The computer is switched on to start Firefox or Explorer - or Chrome - and we spend more time every day on the internet than around the windows.
What is the purpose of half the applications that accompany the system?
Fewer is more.
And Windows 7 has to be less, much less than Vista.
Unhealthy, less laden, less complex.
Microsoft is now interested in offering its services on-on-line and finally becoming an alternative to Google.
Desperately they need it, and I have a small example here.
I took this photo in one rest room for Microsoft employees in Redmond at the beginning of the month.
There was a poster on a beverage vending machine that begs them to use their search tools: "Your search. Our future."
In 2006, an independent study showed that 66 per cent of Microsoft's employees used Google to search the Internet.
Last week, by the way, the delay of the new version of Windows Mobile 7 was delayed.
It does not come at the beginning of 2009, but at the very end, so let’s count until 2010.
At Microsoft, they will certainly try to market Windows 7 and Windows Mobile 7 together.
The phone is becoming more and more a computer.
Due to the fact that Android and the iPhone are already on the market, they will have to pay expensively for delays.
Le Fabuleux Marcel
