Speaking from sleep
Some of the kids are talking at night.
I don’t mean those who wake up and tell us something that happened the day before, but children who talk while they sleep.
When it happens for the first time, it may smash you out, but it's actually insignificant.
It is a harmless sleep disorder, which is called somniocquious.
It is quite frequent, especially for children and adolescents.
I'm sure you've told many of you that at night he was talking from sleep.
This phenomenon can occur very sporadically (e.g. once in a lifetime of high fevers), or with greater regularity (each day, once a week, once a few months...), on the second day the baby does not remember anything.
There’s not just one reason to explain its onset.
It is often associated with other sleep disorders, such as nightmares, nighttime floes, enuresis nocturna (night bedwetting) or sleepwalking, but it does not have to be the case.
As already mentioned earlier, it can often be accompanied by very high fevers, stressful or exaggerated situations.
In any case, it is a low-explored phenomenon.
The intensity with which it appears varies too.
The child can speak with a calm voice, say individual words, whisper, or vice versa (screams, weepings, long speeches...).
In cases where it manifests itself with great strength, it would be advisable to observe the child and consult a specialist to find out if he has any problem that provokes a great anxiety and manifests itself in this way.
We have said that it is a harmless disorder, therefore it is not treated only if it is associated with an additional sleep disorder that can be treated (e.g. if it is associated with apnea syndrome or with permanent stress).
However, we can do this to keep in mind a few very simple preventive measures, such as:
Trying not to upset the child too much before going to sleep.
To look for peaceful games and avoid scary or somewhat violent television programs or series.
For very ner Nervous children and those to whom it is possible to relax, it is possible to use relaxation techniques that will help them to gain a certain inner peace of mind.
This will allow the child to sleep calmer.
When Somnikovice appears, try to remain calm.
Remind yourself that it is harmless to the child and that from what was going to happen, he will no longer remember anything the next day.
Useful advice
Don't be him.
It is useless because it is not conscious and you will interrupt his sleep.
Even when he talks, he sleeps, and therefore rests.
Reminder the fact that it seems to you several times in a row that they are very worried, it would be advisable to turn for advice or to find out what provokes such an anxiection in a child.
If it is to understand what he says (which is not so usual), avoid the temptation to interrogate him and pull information from it.
It won't be useless, 'cause when he's asleep, he's not conscious to organize her thoughts.
In addition, you will hear what he says, but you don't know what he's dreaming about and why he's saying it, so be very careful about how you're interpreting the information and what conclusions you're coming to.
Don’t ever tell a child that you can inquisce on his secret at night.
Not even for fun.
This provokes great anxieties.
Just don’t worry, it doesn’t be bad, it's just an episode that only lasts a few minutes.
