Sarkozy goes towards angry fishermen
Before flying to the United States, Nicolas Sarkozy takes a detachment via Brittany.
In order to face a demonstration of the strength of the fishermen, who on Monday severely eroded the accessibility of several Breton and Norman ports, the French president decided on Tuesday morning to the port of Guilvinec in Finistère to meet their representatives there.
The latter vowed to be accepted "in a constructive spirit" and hope to "journalize with concrete proposals."
The presidential visit comes at the same time as the General Assembly of the "crisis committee" created by Breton fishermen in order to obtain measures against a sharp increase in oil prices, which has peaked exceeding an average of 1.14 euros per litre.
According to Elysée Palace spokesman David Martinon, the president "will talk about solutions that can be imaginable in an effort to help their companies regain competitiveness and better ensure the conditions for employing and paying sailors."
Nicolas Sarkozy is awaited fishermen with intransigence.
On Tuesday in the morning they carried out an operation "snapped" (operations of slow-moving vehicles) in Morbihan and Calvados departments, inflicting almost 10km congestion.
Quirboard's holiday port has also been blocked, but connections to Belle-île weren't interrupted.
A filtration dam has been launched on the Quiberon Peninsula.
Sailors demonstrated their dedication already on Monday, when tires and pallets were fissing right at dawn in front of port fuel depots in Brest and Lorient while demonstrations took place in Concarneau and Saint-Brieuc.
The movement has spread to other harbours on the Atlantic coast and la Manche canal, as to the Sables d'Olonne in Vendée, where traulers remained in the harbor, or Le Havre, where about twenty vessels blocked the operation of merchant ships.
Fishermen from Turballe and Croisic in the Loire-Atlantique area halted a blockade of an important Total refinery in Donges during the evening.
Those from Boulogne-sur-Mer decided to await each other until Wednesday's meeting at the ministry.
In Guilvinec, the "crisis committee" is due to decide on Tuesday on how to continue the journey and its participation in the forthcoming meeting at the Ministry of Agriculture and Fisheries in Paris.
However, some Breton fishermen do not want to be represented by the leaders of the state fisheries department, who did not support their movement.
"The strike will continue" because "fishers will not abandon anything" and "they don't come to Paris," Liliane Cariou, chairwoman of the Fisheries Department in Lorient, said.
Purchasing fishermen, who do not pay a tax on diesel, currently pay around 50 cents per litre, but they believe that the ship's profitability threshold is only reached when the oil is below 0.30 euros per litre.
