Conversation - The Blood That's Sure
Hem-Québec celebrated the tenth anniversaries of the founding yesterday, and its president, Dr. Francine Décary, rightly rejoiced, because Héma-Québec became one of the most reliable and efficient blood suppliers in the world.
First of all, he regained the confidence of the public, which was seriously shaken after the contaminated-bodied blood scandal in the 80's and 90's.
As it is widely known, the state reserve of blood maintained by the Transfusion of the Canadian Red Cross Transfusion Department was contaminated during the 80's by the immune deficiency virus (HIV) virus and the hepatitis C virus.
Due to this drama and the wave of repulsement of the public, the Canadian government in February 1994 launched a public investigation, which was presided over by Judge Horace Krever and aimed at explaining the circumstances that led to such a tragedy and to formulate a recommendation that such a catastrophe should no longer be repetitive.
While Judge Krever has delayed submitting a report, some of the provinces launched a committee aimed to plan a new system of blood management in Canada.
In Québek, the Géline’s committee played this role.
After Gélineau and Krever's report, two contractors were set up in Canada: Héma-Québec, which was supposed to be handled by Québec, and a Canadian blood company for supplying all provinces and both territories.
"While Hema-Québec initiated its activities in 1998, barely 38% of people still had confidence in the Quebec blood system," recalls Dr. Décary.
“The Situation was daunting.
With a staunsive effort to increase the safety of blood products and the constant work in this area over the coming years, we have regained the public’s trust,” he said, adding proudly that at the beginning of 2008, Hém-Québek’s almost 94% québic population trusted Hém-Québek.
Dr. Décary, however, does not lose sight that "confidence is something very fragile".
Since its inception on September 28, 1998, Héma-Québec has applied the prudence principle.
"At that time, in Europe, we were witnessing the occurrence of many cases of the human form of mad cow disease, a new variant of a disease called Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease," says the woman who has led the company since its inception in 1998.
"And we didn't have any idea if this disease could spread with blood.
Therefore, we relinquivied one of the basic recommendations of Krever and Gélineau's report, according to which when we don't know this and when the spread of a serious disease is at stake, in this case Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease, characterized by brain degeneration inevitably leading to death, it is necessary to take an exclusuring measure.
Therefore, in April 1999, we refused blood from people who have stayed in the United Kingdom for more than a month since 1980.
A few years ago it was safely proven that this disease is transmitted through the blood.
Thus, this episode showed us that sticking to the principle of prudence made sense.”
In 1999 there was a much more accurate antivironmentary antivironment technique in the market: nucleic acid (NAT tests), which make it possible to reveal directly the presence of the virus itself in the donor blood, and not the presence of antibodies on which tests were used before.
NAT tests have made it possible to shorten the deafening period, which occurs between the moment the patient's infection and the moment the first viruses can be detected by our techniques in the blood, the specialist explains.
We were able to shorten this period to less than a week.
Significantly reduced the risk that some donors could be infected without being aware of it and not being able to figure it out.
These improvements made it possible to significantly reduce the risk of acquiring AIDS through a blood transfusion.
The risk of getting HIV-contaminated-contaminated blood is now exposed to one in 12 million.
"By other words, it means that before they become infected, 12 million blood products will need to be distributed.
Due to this, Héma-Québec disseminates 400 000 to 500 000 blood products every year, it will be necessary for 24 years before the first case of HIV transmission occurs through a blood product.
“I believe that today we can safely receive or prescrib the transfiction of blood necessary for recovery,” says Hém-Québek, the head of the Hém-Québek leadership.
"The challenge for the next decade is the preservation of the safety of our products.
We never know what kind of disease-fruiting element can appear in the system.
It wasn't last year that the Chikungunya virus appeared on Réunion, which, like West Niles, is transmitted into the blood by sanctuary sanctuary.
It is always necessary to be vigilant," Dr. Décary continues, emphasizing that, in addition to the safe-supplied Hema-Québec must also reckon with sufficient supplies.
“Necessities are growing because the population is aging.
As a result of the more aggressive cancer treatment, patients often need a new dose of red blood cells or platelets.
Aging of the population also has consequences for a network of blood givers, which, on average, consists of only 3% Québecca permissible for donation."
Any healthy person at least 18 years of age, corresponding to the admissibility criteria, but can donate blood only once every 56 days, i.e. six times a year.
On average, 14% of blood-supplied people aren't accepted, because, for example, carriers of AIDS or malaria, and if they've visited a country where mud chills roars are expelled for a year.
“With these 3%, we are able to maintain a condition that meets the needs of hospitals.
Lacks are very rare.
However, it is Necessary to repudiate the members of a network of donors in proportion to how they are aging and they can no longer donate blood.
Convincing people to give blood is difficult, especially to maintain and insulate them to become regular donors.
Life today is so unbridled that we rarely find an hour to don’t don’t donate the blood,” says Francine Décary.
With a one-in-a-half-$297-million-dollar Héma-Québec budget, it delivers not only blood products, but also human tissue as bone transmissuits, cardiac flauffs, ophthalmic eyeballs and skin that is almost exclusively used for major burns.
These tissues are analyzed, processed and freezed before they are deposited in Hém-Québek, which also manages a single public blood bank from the umbilical cord in  Quebec.
The umbilical cord, which was formerly considered biomedical waste, afflict blood-enainted to stem cells, which can be used to treat serious diseases such as leukemia.
“A few weeks ago, we celebrated the deposit of our thousands of blood samples from the umbilical cord to the bank, and we assume that we will collect over a thousand of them a year.
Earlier at the beginning of next year, we should start offering them for transplants,” says Dr. Décaryová, who describes the blood-suppression program from the umbilical cord as very popular.
"Some mums are disappointed that they don't have access to it," because Héma-Québec doesn't have enough resources to offer this comprehensive and expensive program to all hospitals in Quebec.
