The Warsaw Stock Exchange made an offer to buy the Prague Stock Exchange
Two-hundred-hundred-million-eedes – approximately five billion Czech crowns – has been offered by the Warsaw Stock Exchange to buy a one-hundred-hundred-per-hort-perceailing package of shares in the Prague Stock Exchange.
Ludwik Sobolewski, chairman of the Warsaw Securities Stock Exchange, confirmed an offer made by the Polish side even though the Czechs have previously expediated the Warsaw Stock Exchange from the circle of possible new owners of the Prague Stock Exchange.
This is due to the fact that the owner of the Warsaw Stock Exchange is the state.
99 per cent of the shares are in the hands of the Polish state, which favors 60 per cent of securities to be sold in a few months.
As the Czech economic daily E15 writes, the offer of Poles represents the highest amount that has so far been offered to bidders for the Prague Stock Exchange.
According to Czech newspapers, the Viennese Securities Stock Exchange has so far filed an offer, the Transatlantic Stock Exchange Nasdaq, the New York Stock Exchange, the London Stock Exchange and Deutsche Börse.
Petr Koblic, the head of the Prague Stock Exchange, stated last week that the Warsaw Stock Exchange would be the worst-in-company owner of all interested parties and did not rule that this could lead to the demise of the Prague Stock Exchange in the future.
Emphasizing the value of the Prague Securrency Stock Exchange is estimated by stock market experts at 5-10 billion.
According to the shareholder agreement, approximately 90 percent of the shares could be reselled.
The biggest owner of the Prague Stock Exchange is the investment bank Patria Finance with an approximately 25 percent share, followed by Česká spořitelna with 14.7 and Tiger Holding Four S. r. l. with 13.5 per cent.
