An intelligent car impuses a Bumbledoter flight
So what would be the best method of developing technologies capable of preventing a collision, if not an imitation of obstacle detectors occurring in the world of nature?
At bees, for example.
Definitely the staunthlining idea of a surprising project, presented at Japan's CEATEC trade fair, was impressed by Nissan bumblebees. It was a matter of how to build a next-generation raincoundrel-defunit system.
Collaborating with the University of Tokyo, Nissan has engineered biomimimetic robotic robotic control system, a robotized mini-car that is equipped with bee properties, with the ultimate goal of helping to create a system that can eliminate all collisions.
"During the flight, each bee creates its own oval-shaped personal space, which actually closely resembles our safety protective cover," Kazuhiro Doi, CEO of Technology's PR and R&D brand.
An important role is played by folded eyes, which allow bees to be seen in all directions at a viewing angle of more than 300 degrees.
To cocopy the function of the folded mesh, the engineers reached for the idea of a laser rangefinder.
It identifies obstructions at a distance of up to 2 meters in the BR23C field of vision, calculates their distance and sends a signal to the on-board microprocessor, which is immediately transformed into an operation aimed at preventing a collision.
Obstacle is intertwined within a fraction of a second, then the robotized car starts imitating the movements of the bee, and by rotating the wheels within the range needed to prevent the collision immediately changes direction.
Contrary to any existing system, this tempting manoeuvre takes place in an absolutely instinctive way.
“If that wasn’t the case, a robotic car wouldn’t be able to respond quickly enough to avoid obstacles,” explains Toshijuki Ando, Head of Mobility Laboratories and a Robotic Car Project.
It must react instinctively and immediately, as this technology suits the most vulneraguable and invulnerous layer of the safety protective cover, inside which the collision is currently considered unavoidable.
The whole process must reflect what a bee is doing to elevate other bees.
This process must not take any more than the winking of an eye.
Contrary to the bees, this movement can not go up, down or diagonally, it can only be two-dimensional and only in the direction in which the wheels can be rotated.
Instead of the infinite possibilities offered by bees when evading, in the case of a robotic car only the possibility of revolving movement, acceleration and decelerating.
BR23C uses lithium-polymer accumulators that are lighter and have a higher energy density, which means they have a longer service life than lithium-ion variants.
According to Ando, an insidious, intelligent response is important, not the ability to process, much less the storage of data.
This mechanism has to process and respond to only a few incoming signals per second.
There is no need for him to store them and respond using an intuitive logic based on data from previous manoeuvres, so he constantly only uses memory the size of a few kilobytes.
Doesn't need merchandise with a giga- or a mega-baby capacity.
This is the first step towards developing an Immediate-Election-taking technology that is estimated by Nissan could get into mass-produced cars within ten years.
Another possibility of application can be the connection of the unit with the GPS satellite navigation system, which, after being avoided in the path of lying obstacles, would lead the motorist to a specified final destination.
Damned expensive baby already has a name
$700 Billion-of-threshold bail-out package law, which is to be divided into several instalments aimed at repurchasing low-cost loans from banks, has a written form.
In the first round, half the amount will be used.
After further talks, which did not end until Saturday after midnight U.S. Eastern Time, congressional officials reached an agreement in a several-period debate over a $700-billion-year bail-out bailout.
"We have made significant progress," said Nancy Pelosi, Democrat Speaker of the House of Deputies, according to which it remains only to draw up an agreement.
Compromise on the package was also supported by both presidential candidates Barack Obama and John McCain.
Treasury Secretary Henry Paulson said: "The program will work and it will be effective, but further steps need to be taken."
The written form of the package was published before the opening of the Asian markets on Sunday at 8 o'clock in the evening of Washington time (i.e. on Monday at two o'clock in the morning Central European time), but it did not cause a special rise on the stock exchanges.
A draft legal standard hearing in the name of an emergency economic stabilization Act of 2008, to be set up for the program, will be first voted on by the Chamber of Deputies of Representatives on Monday, after which there will be a turn for the Senate.
President George W. Bush interviewed Pelosi on Saturday night, then welcomed news of the agreement at the White House as well.
"We are satisfied with the progress made last night and we very much appreciate the efforts of both sides to stabilise our financial markets and protect our economy," White House spokesman Tony Fratto said.
After all, the program differs from the Bush administration's original proposal, in which it was supposed to have a total of $700 billion to be repurchasing low-cost mortgage-pro-secured securities at the same time.
It is now possible to spend a maximum of $700 billion in the first round of $700 billion, with $250 Billion being used immediately after Congress decides the matter as expected on Monday, the Treasury Department will buy mortgage-secquirce-to-response securities that are rated as low-quality, the fate of the remaining $100 billion will then depend on the president's decision.
The program will be inspected by a special corps, of which the chairman of the Federal Reserve Bank will also be a member.
Expenditure of the second half of $700 billion will depend on Congress's further decision.
The Ministry of Finance can, within the meaning of the law, apply a combined tactic: at the same time it can repurchase low-cluable loans, mortgages and securities secured by mortgages or other "poisonous" loans.
Repurchase can also take place directly as part of the auctions, competent representatives of the ministry said.
The legal standard will allow companies concerned to participate in an insurance program, in which the Ministry of Finance will provide them with a guarantee for funds constituting a cause for concern, for which the company will then pay the price corresponding to the amount of expected damages.
According to Henry Paulson, the law provides the necessary means to spend up to $700 billion to meet the urgent demands of the U.S. financial system, whether it’s a large-scale purchase of bad funds, their insurance, or to prevent systemic risks associated with collapsing one of the large financial institutions.
"We will take the necessary steps so that we are ready to implement the law as soon as it is signed," he added.
On the contrary, the heads of the banks that have run into difficulties and are awaiting the assistance of the state can reckon that the government will reduce their severance pay - which in overseas slang it is simply called a "gold parachute".
Their payments will be limited only in certain cases, moreover, these restrictions will depend on the method of assistance provided by the ministry.
Responsibility basically means that if the ministry purchases as part of an Auction, a tax surreplice will be imposed on gold parachutes, if the fallen directors of the banks are not retired directly.
In the event of direct redemption of low-cluable loans, the government will aggressively intervene in order to ensure that the money for management is not exaggerated.
It has to be added that pre-existing treaties of gold parachutes do not change, the restriction applies only to new treaties, which is supposed to be a further stimulus for participation in the program.
"This is the first case in U.S. history when there will be legal restrictions on the remuneration of director-in-chieves of CEOs," Democrat Barney Frank, Chairman of the Financial Services of the Chamber of Deputies of the House of Representatives.
In Addition, there will allude the state in the banks participating in the program the right to acquire a share, which will reduce the risk of taxpayers, because as soon as the economy and the rescued bank stand on their feet, they can even make a profit on this transaction.
The proposal's plan states that over the next five years, the president must submit a proposal to the Congress determining how taxpayers' expected losses from the financial sector can be refunded.
Of course, there were also voices on both sides expressing in relation to the plan of doubts.
“U.S. citizens didn’t ask us for this, because this won’t protect taxpayers’ dollars,” Daler Issa said.
His Democrat Ohio colleague, Mary Kaptur, believes the provisions have been urged by exactly those responsible for the collapse of Wall Street.
Indusd prices continue to rise
Domestic selling prices in the industry increased by 0.1 percent in August 2008 due to the previous month, compared to August 2007 by 12.9 percent, the Central Statistical Office announced.
Export sales prices in the industry were up 1.1 percent compared to July 2008, down 3.9 per cent year-on-year.
As a result of changes in domestic and export prices compared to the previous month, production prices were higher by 0.7 per cent compared to the previous month, rising by 3.2 percent year-on-year.
Domestic sales prices in the industry rose only minimally due to the previous month, by 0.1 percent.
Domestic sales prices in the manufacturing sector have also rised slightly, by 0.1 per cent compared to July.
The largest price increases took place in August 2008 in the leather and footwear sector (2.8 percent), mainly due to the month-on-month increase in prices in the production sector of bag and saddling products by 4.6 per cent, respectively.
A higher price level of plastic and chemical raw materials caused a significant monthly increase in prices in the production of chemical materials and products (1.6 per cent).
Price reductions occurred in the crude processing sector (1.4 per cent), manufacturing of electrical machinery and instruments (0.9 per cent), as well as - at a minimal extent - in the woodworking sector (0.1 percent).
Prices in the field of paper production and publishing and printing activities have remained unchanged.
In the other sectors of the manufacturing industry, we have projected an increase in prices ranging from 0.2 - 0.9 per cent.
In the electricity, gas and water sectors, which have a great deal of major weight in industrial sales, prices increased by 0.3 per cent in August, mainly due to an officially determined increase in gas prices running since 1 July.
In the twelve-month comparison of domestic sales in individual sectors of the processing industry, prices rose the most in the professional oil processing sector (32.4 percent).
It was possible to observe a 13.2 per cent increase in the production of food products, beverages and tobacco products, which has a decisive weight.
Prices have been deceivable in the textile and textile manufacturing (2.5 per cent) and production of electrical machinery and apparatus (0.3 per cent).
In other sectors of the processing industry, the rate of price growth ranged between 0.6 and 9.1 per cent.
In total, prices in the manufacturing industry have risen 10.2 per cent over the past year.
Prices in the production and distribution of electricity, gas and water sectors surpassed by 18.7 per cent from the previous year.
In terms of dividing according to the focus of the sector, the highest one-per-season price increase (0.7 per cent) was reflected in domestic sales in August in domestic sales.
Prices have risen 0.3 per cent in consumer-compeeding sectors, prices did not change in energy-producing and further processing sectors due to the previous month.
Due to the same month of the previous year in August 2008, the largest price increase could be observed in the energy-processing and further processed sectors (16.8 percent).
In sectors producing consumer goods, we have projected an increase in prices of 6.8 per cent, on the contrary, for tangible assets a decrease of 1.4 per cent.
Export sales prices of industrial products measured in forints increased by 1.1 percent in August compared to the previous month, falling 3.9 per cent in a 12-per-month comparison.
Within a month, our domestic currency weakened by 1.7 per cent relative to the euro, up by 7.1 per cent compared to August 2007, up 7.5 per cent compared to August 2007 and 15.9 percent relative to the dollar.
