This phenomenon manifested itself a few days ago, when the Chamber of Representatives voted against a $700-billion-supplied surrogacy plan presented by the White House: a significant proportion of Americans were more interested and interested in punishing Wall Street than saving economics.
The New York Times also confirms yesterday's New York Times, which informs us that the current crisis erectivates the Republican Party, increasingly distributed among its old East Coast soul, milder, accommodating to federal institutions and a business community, and its new soul, which, on the contrary, is Conservative, hostile to "those out of Washington» and against the world of finance, strong, especially in states in the middle and in the south.
The soul that arrogantly manifested itself in the vote I talked about at the beginning.
However, this just cited thing is a mere example of change, the great changes that the current economic earthquake already shows: not only in the United States, but all over the west, and perhaps not only here.
Both in the U.S. and in Europe, it seems that this crisis appears to act as an acelerator of phenomena that have long been in an incumbent that have been tightening in a new climate and finally come to light.
The first of these phenomena is re-rectuation, an extraordinary revival of the market, the double category State-power in relation to a definite renationalization of the economy.
Indeed, the Crisis is a crisis of banking-to-Financial institutions, which, of course, have strong ties abroad, but in which the budgets of people and families living in a single country, in a single-seater, are primarily included.
Which at the same time created a political problem for those who run it: that is, how to respond to the difficulties and protests of the people and of the families, which are, among other things, the electors.
In short, the crisis appears to be economically world-of-houlder, but politically almost exclusively national.
Political internationality seems substantially out-of-of-horror or that there is nothing much to add: blasphemous evidence was given by the European Union, which, as always, is divided between different interests and various state-state strategies and which cannot decide for one common political line.
And so everyone awaits the intervention of the government of every individual state, rescue plans, recovery of the market, new rules and, most importantly, the distribution of funds: at the moment when we get into the oppression, only the states have economic resources, a number of financial resources, so that they are able to try to put everything in order.
And they also possess the necessary means and the right to be able to use them: two sources of immense value, in certain circumstances that perhaps no market and no international organization will ever be able to dispose to a similar extent.
Moreover, this new emphaphasis, which the crisis puts on a state-national element, is in complete harmony with an ever-greater importance, which is attempting to attribute recent economic advances towards one factor closely associated with it: territorial affiliation.
Paradoxically, in fact, while we were convinced that we had come into the kingdom, an increasingly sophisticated technology, imatrier, while we were convinced that worldwide finances had already predetermined the rule of the world, out of nowhere we realized that, on the contrary, our future would have to deal more and more with such intangible things as water, grain, oil.
All the things that would have been said can be transported from one place on the ground to another, but in any case they are connected, absolutely, to the limited space, the territory.
So whoever becomes their ruler certainly has a lot more cards up his sleeve than he is not, and who is unlucky to live in a grain-of-free place, without oil and without water.
I'm getting to the second news, but it's predicting a turning point.
He who speaks about the territory, the state, the government, inevitably talks about politics, that is, about leadership.
Taking into account the economic crisis, two convergent phenomena appear to be forming.
On the one hand, realizing that there is an objective need for authoritative leadership, a request for someone who would be able to seize the situation.
On the other hand a wave of disaggriticism towards existing leaders, especially economic, which turned out equally unsatisfying and full of dark places.
And together something even deeper and in a definite sense: discarbonation, impatience, a sense of incompetition and indecency trying to dominate the entire ruling class in more and more Western countries.
It seems that they are increasingly expanding in the broad masses of the population, rather than their deeds to be the same as the ideas of the hitherto-popular elite, as their way of feeling and being, as their culture in the true sense of the word that the elite has already surpassed its time and is indifferent to the opinions of the majority.
The demand for leadership simply nourishes one branch from the sub-earth, but a strong movement for the abolition of the ruling classes and the traditional political arrangement manifests itself in unexpected electoral "restrations» or in the unexpected changes in collective moods in the last and most recent times, from the conservative ascension in Austria-Basavsk to the fall of Labour in Great Britain, to the revolbation of American congressmen against Bush.
We have the impression that traditional elites, their sides, their programs, but also their ceremonies, their newspapers, their accredited intelligentsia, someone would say their format, they have more and more problems to comprehension, i.e. to represent, which, from today, is being created in the deep layers of Western societies and that the economic crisis is re-energizing, it is growing, potentially too much.
