Goodbye to the old Paradiso cinema, the cities are disturbed by historic halls
Kinematographic historical halls close in favor of Multiplex.
“A kina-free city is like a blind, it’s like an eyeless face.”
The painting offered by director Giusepp Tornator successfully documents what is going on in major Italian city centers, as well as rural cities.
The process of limiting cinemas, the birth of large multi-shairs in the suburbs and the subsequent demise of monoshals in the central zones of cities, is one of the events that seem inevitable and irreversible, but also has all the hallmarks of a true cultural crime.
Often the historical places packed with memories are disappearing," it is as if we lost a friend, an acquaintance, with whom we shared a long journey and a lot of emotions», feels the director of the New Cinema Paradiso, a film that twenty years ago he won an Oscar for Tornator and a Grand Jury Prize in Cannes, and who respealed the position of an all-a-half cinema and mourn for the way of life.
The values obtained by Anec, the main association of the operators, speak clearly: in the last five years, 316 kinas of the traditional tip have closed the door, and the highest account was paid primarily by the halls in the most important cities: 10 fewer in Florence, about 9 in Milan, Naalapo, Bologna and Rome, 6 fewer in Torink, 5 in Paler.
Emilia-Romagna is first among the regions with a loss of 82 kina rooms, followed by Tuscany (44) and Lombardy (41).
Proof that the first responsibility for their disappearance is the onsltion of the multiplex, it is precisely the three Italian regions, which have registered the highest number of new cyanobacteria multi-share: 195 more canvases in Lombardy over the past five years, both in Tuscany and Emilia-Romagna.
«Italian progression has evolved non-racially» says Paolo Protti, the president of Anecu," it is useful to create new structures, but it would be necessary to guarantee that those already existing will survive, because when one cinema in the city closes, not all its visitors are willing to relocate to a multiplex.
Then let's not even talk about an insufficient subsidy for the fund for the benefit of the operators: the previous national debt is about 10 million euros.
From the point of view of moral and ethical, it is a displeasure that the government disregards existing laws.
To support the sector, between €16 million and €18 a.m.”
Interesting is the Bologna case, the first Italian city, which is worried about closures and which has decided to find a starting point by the signature of the tunnel between the municipality and Anec, the first of this type in Italy for the protection and promotion of small cinemas both in historical centers and in the suburbs.
Among the planned norms are tax-relief actions in favor of operators and benefits for the audience in terms of mobility: spectators will be able to enter a limited-excreation zone and have special prices to halt and in some car parks.
«I like this initiative» says Giuseppe Tornatore" and I hope that other towns will follow the example of Bologly introducing other measures».
However, it is certain that in order to stand up against the great power of a multiplex, traditional halls have to improve the quality of sound and image, because today's audience is more challenging.
Defeating a traditional cinema is a theme that has always been annoying: the closure of the Super Cinema in Bagheria, instead of my childhood and adolescence, it was a spark that convinced me to start "New Paradiso Cinema", which I have been thinking about for 10 years.
I've lived in Rome for 25 years, and when I've been walking in front of an already closed Rialt or Quirinal on Nazionale, where I've shared enough emotion so I wouldn't have to look at dust-stricken signs, I turn to the other side".
