When energy is born from the waves, the big ones come into play.
It's an infinite deposit that covers more than 70 per cent of the Earth's surface and is now starting to be discovered by the world's major energy companies.
Wools, currents, tides are new sources of unrefuscinable energy on which the eyes of colossians like Chevron, which is now trying to stretch their hands to these sources.
Opportunity to create energy from sea movements has been studied for more than a hundred years, and in the 70's in connection with the first oil crisis, research has intensified.
When the price of crude oil declined around 1980, the idea of utilizing sea energy went aside. But now the environmental organization and a new increase in the price of crude oil gave the sector a new impetus.
World Energy Council (WEC) in London stated in 2007 that around 15 percent of the world's electricity consumption could be covered by the use of seawave movements.
this amount would be twice the energy currently produced in nuclear centers.
In addition, there are currents and tides.
The giants are attacking.
